{"parentUuid":"3b9b3564-7258-4cbc-8997-ed99058b92e2","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"f2a0cc15-d78b-4480-864b-d354e2a8f02f","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"linked-kindling-frog","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0166zP31UMpRZpgwBr59XwcZ","type":"tool_result","content":"============================================================\nVERIFICATION OF REDACTED PDFs\n============================================================\n\n### PAPER 1 - First Page Sample ###\n\nMetadata:\n  Title: Anonymized Submission\n  Author: \n  Creator: Anonymized\n\nFirst 1000 characters of text:\nPreprint\nVOICE EVALUATION OF REASONING ABILITY: DIAG-\nNOSING THE MODALITY-INDUCED PERFORMANCE GAP\nYueqian Lin♠♣, Zhengmian Hu♣, Qinsi Wang♠♣, Yudong Liu♠, Hengfan Zhang♠,\nJayakumar Subramanian♣, Nikos Vlassis♣, Hai “Helen” Li♠, Yiran Chen♠\n♠Duke University, Durham, NC, USA\n♣Adobe, San Jose, CA, USA\nCorrespondence: {yueqian.lin@duke.edu, zhengmianh@adobe.com}\nABSTRACT\nWe present Voice Evaluation of Reasoning Ability (VERA), a benchmark for\nevaluating reasoning ability in voice-interactive systems under real-time conver-\nsational constraints. VERA comprises 2,931 voice-native episodes derived from\nestablished text benchmarks and organized into five tracks (Math, Web, Science,\nLong-Context, Factual). Each item is adapted for speech interaction while pre-\nserving reasoning difficulty. VERA enables direct text–voice comparison within\nmodel families and supports analysis of how architectural choices affect reliability.\nWe assess 12 contemporary voice systems alongside strong text baselines a\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### PAPER 2 - First Page Sample ###\n\nMetadata:\n  Title: Anonymized Submission\n  Author: \n  Creator: Anonymized\n\nFirst 1000 characters of text:\nSinging Voice Data Scaling-up:\nAn Introduction to ACE-Opencpop and ACE-KiSing\nJiatong Shi1,∗, Yueqian Lin2,∗, Xinyi Bai3,4, Keyi Zhang, Yuning Wu5, Yuxun Tang5, Yifeng Yu6, Qin\nJin5,⋆, Shinji Watanabe1\n1 Carnegie Mellon University, 2 Duke Kunshan University, 3 Cornell University, 4 Multimodal Art\nProjection Community, 5 Renmin University of China, 6 Georgia Institute of Technology\njiatongs@cs.cmu.edu\nAbstract\nIn singing voice synthesis (SVS), generating singing voices\nfrom musical scores faces challenges due to limited data avail-\nability. This study proposes a unique strategy to address the data\nscarcity in SVS. We employ an existing singing voice synthe-\nsizer for data augmentation, complemented by detailed manual\ntuning, an approach not previously explored in data curation, to\nreduce instances of unnatural voice synthesis. This innovative\nmethod has led to the creation of two expansive singing voice\ndatasets, ACE-Opencpop and ACE-KiSing, which are instru-\nmental for large-scale, mul\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### PAPER 3 - First Page Sample ###\n\nMetadata:\n  Title: Anonymized Submission\n  Author: \n  Creator: Anonymized\n\nFirst 1000 characters of text:\nSPEECHPRUNE: Context-aware Token Pruning for Speech Information Retrieval\nYueqian Lin * 1 Yuzhe Fu * 1 Jingyang Zhang 1 Yudong Liu 1 Jianyi Zhang 1 Jingwei Sun 1 Hai Li 1\nYiran Chen 1\nAbstract\nWhile current Speech Large Language Models\n(Speech LLMs) excel at short-form tasks, they\nstruggle with the computational and representa-\ntional demands of longer audio clips. To advance\nthe model’s capabilities with long-form speech,\nwe introduce Speech Information Retrieval (SIR),\na long-context task for Speech LLMs, and present\nSPIRAL, a 1,012-sample benchmark testing mod-\nels’ ability to extract critical details from long\nspoken inputs. To overcome the challenges of\nprocessing long speech sequences, we propose\nSPEECHPRUNE, a training-free token pruning\nstrategy that uses speech-text similarity and ap-\nproximated attention scores to efficiently discard\nirrelevant tokens. In SPIRAL, SPEECHPRUNE\nachieves accuracy improvements of 29% and up\nto 47% over the original model and the random\npruning mod\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nVerification complete!\n============================================================","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5aa2be5c-7cb7-469c-bf7d-ffbb52590e5c","timestamp":"2026-01-29T04:38:43.603Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"============================================================\nVERIFICATION OF REDACTED PDFs\n============================================================\n\n### PAPER 1 - First Page Sample ###\n\nMetadata:\n  Title: Anonymized Submission\n  Author: \n  Creator: Anonymized\n\nFirst 1000 characters of text:\nPreprint\nVOICE EVALUATION OF REASONING ABILITY: DIAG-\nNOSING THE MODALITY-INDUCED PERFORMANCE GAP\nYueqian Lin♠♣, Zhengmian Hu♣, Qinsi Wang♠♣, Yudong Liu♠, Hengfan Zhang♠,\nJayakumar Subramanian♣, Nikos Vlassis♣, Hai “Helen” Li♠, Yiran Chen♠\n♠Duke University, Durham, NC, USA\n♣Adobe, San Jose, CA, USA\nCorrespondence: {yueqian.lin@duke.edu, zhengmianh@adobe.com}\nABSTRACT\nWe present Voice Evaluation of Reasoning Ability (VERA), a benchmark for\nevaluating reasoning ability in voice-interactive systems under real-time conver-\nsational constraints. VERA comprises 2,931 voice-native episodes derived from\nestablished text benchmarks and organized into five tracks (Math, Web, Science,\nLong-Context, Factual). Each item is adapted for speech interaction while pre-\nserving reasoning difficulty. VERA enables direct text–voice comparison within\nmodel families and supports analysis of how architectural choices affect reliability.\nWe assess 12 contemporary voice systems alongside strong text baselines a\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### PAPER 2 - First Page Sample ###\n\nMetadata:\n  Title: Anonymized Submission\n  Author: \n  Creator: Anonymized\n\nFirst 1000 characters of text:\nSinging Voice Data Scaling-up:\nAn Introduction to ACE-Opencpop and ACE-KiSing\nJiatong Shi1,∗, Yueqian Lin2,∗, Xinyi Bai3,4, Keyi Zhang, Yuning Wu5, Yuxun Tang5, Yifeng Yu6, Qin\nJin5,⋆, Shinji Watanabe1\n1 Carnegie Mellon University, 2 Duke Kunshan University, 3 Cornell University, 4 Multimodal Art\nProjection Community, 5 Renmin University of China, 6 Georgia Institute of Technology\njiatongs@cs.cmu.edu\nAbstract\nIn singing voice synthesis (SVS), generating singing voices\nfrom musical scores faces challenges due to limited data avail-\nability. This study proposes a unique strategy to address the data\nscarcity in SVS. We employ an existing singing voice synthe-\nsizer for data augmentation, complemented by detailed manual\ntuning, an approach not previously explored in data curation, to\nreduce instances of unnatural voice synthesis. This innovative\nmethod has led to the creation of two expansive singing voice\ndatasets, ACE-Opencpop and ACE-KiSing, which are instru-\nmental for large-scale, mul\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### PAPER 3 - First Page Sample ###\n\nMetadata:\n  Title: Anonymized Submission\n  Author: \n  Creator: Anonymized\n\nFirst 1000 characters of text:\nSPEECHPRUNE: Context-aware Token Pruning for Speech Information Retrieval\nYueqian Lin * 1 Yuzhe Fu * 1 Jingyang Zhang 1 Yudong Liu 1 Jianyi Zhang 1 Jingwei Sun 1 Hai Li 1\nYiran Chen 1\nAbstract\nWhile current Speech Large Language Models\n(Speech LLMs) excel at short-form tasks, they\nstruggle with the computational and representa-\ntional demands of longer audio clips. To advance\nthe model’s capabilities with long-form speech,\nwe introduce Speech Information Retrieval (SIR),\na long-context task for Speech LLMs, and present\nSPIRAL, a 1,012-sample benchmark testing mod-\nels’ ability to extract critical details from long\nspoken inputs. To overcome the challenges of\nprocessing long speech sequences, we propose\nSPEECHPRUNE, a training-free token pruning\nstrategy that uses speech-text similarity and ap-\nproximated attention scores to efficiently discard\nirrelevant tokens. In SPIRAL, SPEECHPRUNE\nachieves accuracy improvements of 29% and up\nto 47% over the original model and the random\npruning mod\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\n============================================================\nVerification complete!\n============================================================","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3b9b3564-7258-4cbc-8997-ed99058b92e2"}
{"parentUuid":"5aa2be5c-7cb7-469c-bf7d-ffbb52590e5c","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"f2a0cc15-d78b-4480-864b-d354e2a8f02f","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"linked-kindling-frog","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_vrtx_01RB5z2CKj2dZyU4c6gW7yiN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can see that the text is still visible in the extraction, which means the redaction was done visually (by drawing black rectangles over the text) but the underlying text layer still exists. This is a common issue with visual redaction. Let me create an enhanced version that properly removes the text from the PDF, not just covers it with black boxes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1815,"cache_read_input_tokens":48614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1815,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb3xZiWTT4MvGaLHeJFu","type":"assistant","uuid":"790fe2dd-3739-4d6f-bf8d-7c20d1610aaf","timestamp":"2026-01-29T04:38:47.740Z"}
{"parentUuid":"790fe2dd-3739-4d6f-bf8d-7c20d1610aaf","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"f2a0cc15-d78b-4480-864b-d354e2a8f02f","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"linked-kindling-frog","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_vrtx_01RB5z2CKj2dZyU4c6gW7yiN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01DqGcAbUtEPLGGxXDdVUf1h","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/root/anonymize_pdfs_v2.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nPDF Anonymization Script v2\nProperly redacts and removes author information, affiliations, arXiv IDs, venue information\nUses redaction annotations that remove the underlying text\n\"\"\"\n\nimport fitz  # PyMuPDF\nimport re\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef anonymize_metadata(doc):\n    \"\"\"Remove identifying metadata from PDF\"\"\"\n    metadata = {\n        'author': '',\n        'creator': 'Anonymized',\n        'producer': 'Anonymized',\n        'title': 'Anonymized Submission',\n        'subject': '',\n        'keywords': '',\n    }\n    doc.set_metadata(metadata)\n\n    # Remove XMP metadata if present\n    doc.set_xml_metadata('')\n\ndef get_redaction_patterns():\n    \"\"\"Define patterns to search and redact\"\"\"\n    patterns = {\n        # Paper 1 specific\n        'paper1': {\n            'authors': [\n                'Yueqian Lin', 'Zhengmian Hu', 'Qinsi Wang', 'Yudong Liu',\n                'Hengfan Zhang', 'Jayakumar Subramanian', 'Nikos Vlassis',\n                'Hai \"Helen\" Li', 'Hai Helen Li', 'Yiran Chen'\n            ],\n            'affiliations': [\n                'Duke University', 'Adobe', 'Durham, NC', 'San Jose, CA',\n                'duke.edu', 'adobe.com', 'Durham', 'San Jose'\n            ],\n            'arxiv': [\n                'arXiv:2509.26542', '2509.26542', 'arxiv.org/abs/2509.26542',\n                'https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.26542v1', 'arXiv.org', '2509.26542v1'\n            ],\n            'emails': [\n                'yueqian.lin@duke.edu', 'zhengmianh@adobe.com'\n            ],\n            'symbols': ['♠', '♣']  # affiliation markers\n        },\n        # Paper 2 specific\n        'paper2': {\n            'authors': [\n                'Jiatong Shi', 'Yueqian Lin', 'Xinyi Bai', 'Keyi Zhang',\n                'Yuning Wu', 'Yuxun Tang', 'Yifeng Yu', 'Qin Jin',\n                'Shinji Watanabe'\n            ],\n            'affiliations': [\n                'Carnegie Mellon University', 'Duke Kunshan University',\n                'Cornell University', 'Multimodal Art Projection Community',\n                'Renmin University of China', 'Georgia Institute of Technology',\n                'cmu.edu', 'cs.cmu.edu'\n            ],\n            'venue': [\n                'INTERSPEECH', 'Interspeech', 'ISCA'\n            ],\n            'emails': [\n                'jiatongs@cs.cmu.edu'\n            ]\n        },\n        # Paper 3 specific\n        'paper3': {\n            'authors': [\n                'Yueqian Lin', 'Yuzhe Fu', 'Jingyang Zhang', 'Yudong Liu',\n                'Jianyi Zhang', 'Jingwei Sun', 'Hai Li', 'Yiran Chen'\n            ],\n            'affiliations': [\n                'Duke University', 'Durham', 'USA', 'duke.edu'\n            ],\n            'emails': [\n                'yl768@duke.edu', 'yl768'\n            ]\n        }\n    }\n\n    return patterns\n\ndef anonymize_pdf(input_path, output_path, paper_id):\n    \"\"\"Main function to anonymize a single PDF using proper redaction\"\"\"\n    print(f\"\\nProcessing {input_path}...\")\n\n    # Open the PDF\n    doc = fitz.open(input_path)\n\n    # Anonymize metadata\n    print(\"  - Removing metadata...\")\n    anonymize_metadata(doc)\n\n    # Get patterns for this paper\n    patterns = get_redaction_patterns()\n    paper_patterns = patterns.get(paper_id, {})\n\n    # Combine all patterns into a single list\n    all_patterns = []\n    for category, pattern_list in paper_patterns.items():\n        all_patterns.extend(pattern_list)\n\n    print(f\"  - Searching for {len(all_patterns)} patterns across {len(doc)} pages...\")\n\n    # Process each page\n    total_redactions = 0\n    for page_num in range(len(doc)):\n        page = doc[page_num]\n\n        # Search for each pattern and add redaction annotations\n        for pattern in all_patterns:\n            try:\n                areas = page.search_for(pattern)\n                for area in areas:\n                    # Add redaction annotation (this will remove the text when applied)\n                    page.add_redact_annot(area, text=\"\", fill=(0, 0, 0))\n                    total_redactions += 1\n            except Exception as e:\n                # Silently continue on errors\n                pass\n\n        # Search for email patterns using regex\n        text = page.get_text()\n        email_pattern = re.compile(r'\\b[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\\.[A-Z|a-z]{2,}\\b')\n        emails = email_pattern.findall(text)\n\n        for email in emails:\n            areas = page.search_for(email)\n            for area in areas:\n                page.add_redact_annot(area, text=\"\", fill=(0, 0, 0))\n                total_redactions += 1\n\n        # Search for arXiv patterns\n        arxiv_patterns = [\n            re.compile(r'arXiv:\\d{4}\\.\\d{4,5}(v\\d+)?', re.IGNORECASE),\n            re.compile(r'\\b\\d{4}\\.\\d{4,5}v?\\d*\\b'),\n            re.compile(r'arxiv\\.org/[^\\s,\\)]+', re.IGNORECASE)\n        ]\n\n        for arxiv_re in arxiv_patterns:\n            matches = arxiv_re.findall(text)\n            for match in matches:\n                # Handle tuple results from regex groups\n                if isinstance(match, tuple):\n                    match = ''.join(match)\n                areas = page.search_for(match)\n                for area in areas:\n                    page.add_redact_annot(area, text=\"\", fill=(0, 0, 0))\n                    total_redactions += 1\n\n        # Apply all redactions on this page\n        page.apply_redactions(images=fitz.PDF_REDACT_IMAGE_NONE)\n\n    print(f\"  - Total redactions applied: {total_redactions}\")\n\n    # Save the anonymized PDF\n    print(f\"  - Saving to {output_path}...\")\n    doc.save(output_path, garbage=4, deflate=True, clean=True)\n    doc.close()\n\n    print(f\"  ✓ Successfully anonymized {input_path}\")\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"Main function to process all three papers\"\"\"\n    # Create output directory\n    output_dir = Path(\"/root/redacted\")\n    output_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    print(f\"Created output directory: {output_dir}\")\n\n    # Process each paper\n    papers = [\n        (\"/root/paper1.pdf\", \"/root/redacted/paper1.pdf\", \"paper1\"),\n        (\"/root/paper2.pdf\", \"/root/redacted/paper2.pdf\", \"paper2\"),\n        (\"/root/paper3.pdf\", \"/root/redacted/paper3.pdf\", \"paper3\"),\n    ]\n\n    for input_path, output_path, paper_id in papers:\n        if os.path.exists(input_path):\n            try:\n                anonymize_pdf(input_path, output_path, paper_id)\n            except Exception as e:\n                print(f\"ERROR processing {input_path}: {e}\")\n                import traceback\n                traceback.print_exc()\n        else:\n            print(f\"WARNING: {input_path} not found, skipping...\")\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\"*60)\n    print(\"Anonymization complete!\")\n    print(\"=\"*60)\n    print(f\"\\nRedacted PDFs saved to: {output_dir}\")\n    print(\"\\nPlease review the redacted PDFs to ensure all identifying\")\n    print(\"information has been properly removed.\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1815,"cache_read_input_tokens":48614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1815,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb3xZiWTT4MvGaLHeJFu","type":"assistant","uuid":"512eaa60-9